{"candidates":[{"claim":"A Genetic Algorithm (GA) can find a 45-comparator 13-input sorting network by optimizing comparator placements based on the 0-1 principle.","counterconditions":["The search space is too rugged, causing the GA to converge prematurely on a locally optimal but globally non-sorting network.","The overhead of evaluating 8192 vectors for every individual in the population makes the GA too slow to be practical for a large number of generations."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"6050cf69f391fa33"},{"block":"843819388191d0e9"}],"mechanism":"Initialize a population of 45-comparator networks. Use fitness proportional to the number of 0-1 vectors sorted out of 8192. Apply mutation (changing one comparator) and single-point crossover. The GA is expected to outperform random search by preserving and recombining partially sorting sub-networks.","typicality":0.8},{"claim":"A SAT-based exact search with structural symmetry breaking can prove or disprove the existence of a 44-comparator sorting network.","counterconditions":["The state space for 44 comparators on 13 channels is too large for modern SAT solvers to close within reasonable time limits.","Symmetry breaking is insufficient to prune the search space to a tractable size, leaving the SAT solver stuck on hard unsatisfiable instances."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"bfc42b3064ed02fe"},{"block":"8a7a7d8f89f48b3e"}],"mechanism":"Encode the sorting network as a boolean satisfiability problem where variables represent the existence of comparators between channels at specific layers. Add clauses enforcing the 0-1 principle and symmetry breaking based on channel reflection (i -> n-1-i) to prune the search space.","typicality":0.6},{"claim":"Extending the optimal 12-input network (39 comparators) by inserting the 13th channel and adding at most 6 comparators will yield a valid 45-comparator network.","counterconditions":["The 13th channel introduces inversions that require more than 6 comparators to resolve, exceeding the 45-comparator budget.","Inserting comparators for the 13th channel disrupts the existing optimal 12-input sorting order, requiring a complete rebuild rather than an extension."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"8a7a7d8f89f48b3e"},{"block":"1194dff78a2795cf"}],"mechanism":"Take the known optimal 39-comparator 12-input network. Introduce channel 12. Systematically insert comparators involving channel 12 to route the new element to its correct sorted position without disturbing the relative order of the first 12 channels, aiming for a total of 45 comparators.","typicality":0.4},{"claim":"A pruning rule based on reachable-set shrink schedules will enable exhaustive depth-first search to find a 44-comparator network by eliminating dead branches early.","counterconditions":["The computational overhead of calculating reachable sets for every node exceeds the time saved by pruning branches.","The pruning rule fails to reduce the branching factor sufficiently, leaving the search tree effectively infinite."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"4f053d098dcef347"},{"block":"817aa2704ec43646"}],"mechanism":"During depth-first search of comparator placements, compute the set of reachable output patterns. If a partial network leaves a specific output pattern unreachable within the remaining depth budget, prune the branch immediately.","typicality":0.3}],"research_proposals":[{"purpose":"To verify the published best known bounds for S(13) and check if S(13)=45 has been proven or remains open, as well as to confirm if Batcher's construction indeed yields 48 comparators for n=13.","request_identifier":"res_001_sorting_network_bounds","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_network"]}],"scratch_proposal":{"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Exploring the idea of using graph neural networks (GNNs) to predict promising comparator placements based on partial networks. The state of a sorting network can be represented as a graph, and a GNN could potentially learn heuristics that outperform traditional human-designed pruning rules.","possible_next_move":"Design a small GNN model that takes a partial network and outputs probabilities for the next comparator placement. Train it via reinforcement learning on smaller n (e.g., n=9, n=10) and test its transferability to n=13.","why_keep_this":"Machine learning approaches to combinatorial optimization have shown promise in related problems (e.g., SAT solving, traveling salesperson). It might provide a novel structural hypothesis for why search stalls at 45."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"If the optimal 13-input network has 44 comparators, what is the structural reason that it evades all known search strategies? Is there a specific type of comparator pattern that is systematically avoided by current symmetry-breaking rules?"}]},"simulation_proposals":[{"declared_assumptions":["Evaluating 8192 vectors is the fitness function.","Population size and generation count are bounded for runtime.","A fixed budget of 5000 network evaluations is used for both."],"discriminating_purpose":"To determine if a GA is a viable strategy for finding 45-comparator networks before committing to exhaustive or SAT-based methods.","hypothesis":"A Genetic Algorithm with fitness proportional to the number of sorted 0-1 vectors will find a fully sorting 45-comparator network significantly faster (in fewer evaluations) than a purely random search.","interpretation_conditions":["If GA reaches 8192 and random does not, GA is favored.","If neither reaches 8192, neither strategy is efficient enough under the given budget.","If random search reaches 8192, the problem is easier than expected or the budget is too large."],"model_source":"import random\n\ndef evaluate_network(net, n=13):\n    max_v = 1 << n\n    score = 0\n    for v in range(max_v):\n        bits = [(v >> k) & 1 for k in range(n)]\n        for i, j in net:\n            if bits[i] > bits[j]:\n                bits[i], bits[j] = bits[j], bits[i]\n        if all(bits[i] <= bits[i+1] for i in range(n-1)):\n            score += 1\n    return score\n\ndef random_search(n=13, L=45, max_evals=5000):\n    best_score = 0\n    for _ in range(max_evals):\n        net = []\n        for _ in range(L):\n            i, j = random.randint(0, n-1), random.randint(0, n-1)\n            if i > j: i, j = j, i\n            if i == j: j = (i+1)%n\n            net.append((i, j))\n        score = evaluate_network(net, n)\n        if score > best_score:\n            best_score = score\n        if best_score == 1 << n:\n            break\n    return best_score\n\ndef ga_search(n=13, L=45, pop_size=20, generations=250):\n    population = []\n    for _ in range(pop_size):\n        net = []\n        for _ in range(L):\n            i, j = random.randint(0, n-1), random.randint(0, n-1)\n            if i > j: i, j = j, i\n            if i == j: j = (i+1)%n\n            net.append((i, j))\n        population.append(net)\n\n    best_score = 0\n    for _ in range(generations):\n        scores = [evaluate_network(p, n) for p in population]\n        if max(scores) > best_score:\n            best_score = max(scores)\n        if best_score == 1 << n:\n            break\n        new_pop = []\n        for _ in range(pop_size):\n            t1, t2 = random.sample(range(pop_size), 2)\n            p1 = population[t1] if scores[t1] > scores[t2] else population[t2]\n            t3, t4 = random.sample(range(pop_size), 2)\n            p2 = population[t3] if scores[t3] > scores[t4] else population[t4]\n            child = p1[:L//2] + p2[L//2:]\n            if random.random() < 0.2:\n                idx = random.randint(0, L-1)\n                i, j = random.randint(0, n-1), random.randint(0, n-1)\n                if i > j: i, j = j, i\n                if i == j: j = (i+1)%n\n                child[idx] = (i, j)\n            new_pop.append(child)\n        population = new_pop\n    return best_score\n\nrandom.seed(42)\nrs_score = random_search(max_evals=5000)\nrandom.seed(42)\nga_score = ga_search(pop_size=20, generations=250)\nprint(f\"Random search score: {rs_score} / {1<<13}\")\nprint(f\"GA search score: {ga_score} / {1<<13}\")\n","parameter_definitions":[{"name":"max_evals","values_json":"5000"}],"request_identifier":"sim_001_ga_vs_random","requested_observables":["max_fitness_ga","max_fitness_random"],"rival_predictions":["GA reaches a score of 8192/8192 in under 5,000 evaluations.","Random search remains strictly below 8192/8192 after 5,000 evaluations."],"simulation_mode":"sandboxed_python_v1"}]}